in a
weird white dwarf heading out into the

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.040
depths of space

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.799
okay so a paper came out on the 20th of
june

00:00:14.799 --> 00:00:18.880
so relatively recently here it is sdss
and then the long telephone number that

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.560
tells you what the star is
the partially burned remnant of a low

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.160
mass white dwarf that underwent
thermonuclear ignition question mark so

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.359
I think we need to back up a little bit
and talk about so this is a white dwarf

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:32.399
star
and it's associated with a particular

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:37.040
type of supernova
uh thing called a type 1a supernova very

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.239
famous type of supernova because it's
used as a standard candle something

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:42.239
which is the same brightness wherever
you see it

00:00:42.239 --> 00:00:45.280
so it's used for cosmology where we can
actually use it and figure out how far

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.840
things are away from how bright it
appears

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.120
the reason why it's a standard candle is
because of the mode

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.480
the way in which it explodes and
essentially what happens is you have a

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.640
white dwarf
which is a very compact star it's held

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.879
up only by the pressure of
quantum mechanics effectively the always

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:05.680
the question with the star is why it
doesn't just collapse under its own

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.119
gravity when it's
burning nuclear processes in its core

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:11.439
that provides the energy source that
keeps it up

00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:15.119
but the end of its life that goes away
and the star will collapse

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:18.479
and so for example for a white dwarf
it'll collapse right down to something

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:22.640
about the size of the earth
at that point these weird quantum

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.640
mechanical effects kick in and stop it
collapsing any further but what can then

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.119
happen
there is a maximum mass for which that

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:32.799
effect will work
thing called the chandrasekhar mass and

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:35.680
if you keep adding
in some way more and more mass to the

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.159
start eventually you're going to
approach that mass

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:41.280
and there's a danger that the thing will
collapse further

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.880
and actually all sorts of turmoil will
occur within the white dwarf star it'll

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.000
become very convective it'll become very
disturbed inside

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.560
that's the point at which white dwarf is
in danger of actually exploding

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.680
by firing off a whole bunch of new
nuclear

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.119
thermonuclear processes but in a very
uncontrolled way

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:03.200
and so it will essentially explode like
a bomb

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.960
interestingly the the views on this have
changed it used to be thought that when

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.920
a star tipped over
that magical chandrasekhar mass that was

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.840
the point at which it blew up
as a supernova it's now thought it never

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.440
quite gets to that mass but as it
approaches that mass as i say it becomes

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.640
very disturbed
stirs up very energetic processes can

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.080
start happening and something
then kicks off these uh nuclear

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.160
explosions within it so then the
question is

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.840
what why would a star be putting on
weight where's its extra mass coming

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.959
from
and in order for that to happen there

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:37.280
has to be another star nearby
to kind of donate the extra mass and so

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.160
it's probably in systems where there's a
binary you've got two

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.920
two stars orbiting each other and one of
them is donating

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.160
some of its mass to the white dwarf and
so eventually it reaches to this point

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.959
where
these runaway nuclear processes occur

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:53.519
and it explodes
so sort of skimming matter from its

00:02:53.519 --> 00:02:57.040
next-door neighbor it's sufficiently
close to it it's actually ripping the

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:00.319
mass off it it's you know it's
its pull of gravity becomes stronger

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:03.840
than the pull of gravity in the star
that was holding onto that matter to

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.159
start with
so it's literally ripping off the outer

00:03:06.159 --> 00:03:11.040
layers of its of its neighboring star
and acquiring them for itself usually

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.760
that process
is thought to be sufficiently disruptive

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.720
that there's nothing left at the end
that the star just literally blows

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.800
itself to bits
the interesting case here is that here

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.760
there is still a white dwarf left
but it has some weird properties and in

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.080
particular when they studied the spectra
of it

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.280
when they split the light up into the
colours of the rainbow and looked at

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.560
the sort of signature of the different
different chemical elements

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.480
they found some of those heavier
elements that are made in the supernova

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.200
explosions
so it looks like some sort of

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.159
thermonuclear process has occurred with
this white dwarf

00:03:44.159 --> 00:03:47.360
but it clearly hasn't been quite as
destructive as it usually is because

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.120
there's still a white dwarf left at the
end

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.480
and the other interesting thing is that
the nuclear processes haven't gone all

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.879
the way
to the sort of end state that they

00:03:54.879 --> 00:03:59.360
usually do usually you end up
producing you know so the the carbon in

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:02.400
the white dwarf
ends up producing silicon and silicon

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.640
turns into nickel and the nickel turns
into iron

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.560
there's a whole series of nuclear
reactions that go on and those produce

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.760
all the elements up to iron the
interesting thing about

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.599
this white dwarf is it doesn't have
those heaviest elements in it it doesn't

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:17.680
have iron in it it doesn't have nickel
in it

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.799
so it looks like the process was sort of
incomplete

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:24.320
it didn't destroy the star and it didn't
go through all the nuclear reactions

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.240
that these things usually go through
so it's like a failed supernova it

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.520
didn't quite fail clearly something
pretty dramatic happened to it

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.520
but it didn't quite go all the way so
it's sort of yeah it didn't quite

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.040
detonate completely it was an incomplete
detonation the other interesting thing

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.199
about
it this particular star is they can

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:44.560
measure its speed you can measure the
doppler shift to tell you it's speed

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.600
along the line of sight but actually
there are also proper motion

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.600
measurements for it they're also
measurements of its

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.440
its changing position on the sky so they
know how fast it's going

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.800
and it's actually it's going around the
wrong way it's not orbiting around the

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.440
milky way in the same direction as most
